st of goods available for sale = $2,000 + $1,000 + $500 + $1,500 + $8,000\n   = $13,000\n   ```\n\n2. Calculate the cost of goods sold:\n   ```\n   Cost of goods sold = Cost of goods available for sale - Ending inventory\n   = $13,000 - $3,000\n   = $10,000\n   ```\n\nIf the numbers were not aligned, you might incorrectly add or subtract the units, leading to an incorrect cost of goods available for sale or cost of goods sold. Proper alignment ensures that the calculations are performed correctly, resulting in the accurate inventory valuation.\n\nThis example demonstrates how aligning numbers by their place values is crucial in financial audits involving inventory valuation for a manufacturing company for ensuring the accuracy and precision of the calculations, which is essential for the financial health and reporting of the company.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>In a real-world scenario, aligning numbers by their place values is crucial in a financial audit involving inventory valuation for a manufacturing company. For instance, consider an auditor reviewing a manufacturing company's financial statements for inventory valuation. Suppose the company has the following financial data:\n- Cost of goods sold (COGS): $10,000\n- Beginning inventory: $2,000\n- Ending inventory: $3,000\n- Raw materials inventory: $1,000\n- Work-in-progress inventory: $500\n- Finished goods inventory: $1,500\n\nTo calculate the cost of goods available for sale and the cost of goods sold, the auditor would align the numbers by their place values and perform the calculations:\n\n1. Calculate the cost of goods available for sale:\n   ```\n   Cost of goods available for sale = Beginning inventory + Raw materials inventory + Work-in-progress inventory + Finished goods inventory + Purchases\n   ```\n   (Assuming purchases are $8,000 for simplicity)\n   ```\n   Cost of goods available for sale = $2,000 + $1,000 + $500 + $1,500 + $8,000\n   = $13,000\n   ```\n\n2. Calculate the cost of goods sold:\n   ```