ngle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one triangular base} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n\\]\nGiven:\n- Base of the triangle \\( b = 6 \\) units\n- Height of the triangle \\( h = 4 \\) units\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one triangular base} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times 4 = 12 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nSince there are two triangular bases, the total area of the two bases is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of the two triangular bases} = 2 \\times 12 = 24 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the area of the three rectangular lateral faces\nThe three rectangular faces are formed by the height of the prism and the sides of the triangular base.\n\n1. **First rectangular face:**\n   - Side length = Base of the triangle = 6 units\n   - Height of the prism = 10 units\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = 6 \\times 10 = 60 \\text{ square units}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Second rectangular face:**\n   - Side length = Height of the triangle = 4 units\n   - Height of the prism = 10 units\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = 4 \\times 10 = 40 \\text{ square units}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Third rectangular face:**\n   - Side length = Remaining side of the triangle (using Pythagorean theorem to find the hypotenuse of the right triangle formed by the height and half the base)\n   - Hypotenuse \\( c = \\sqrt{6^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 16} = \\sqrt{52} = 2\\sqrt{13} \\)\n   - Height of the prism = 10 units\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = 2\\sqrt{13} \\times 10 = 20\\sqrt{13} \\text{ square units}\n   \\]\n\n### Step 3: Sum the areas of all faces\n\\[\n\\text{Total surface area} = \\text{Total area of the two triangular bases} + \\text{Area of the three rectangular faces}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Total surface area} = 24 + 60 + 40 + 20\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo, the total surface area of the triangular prism is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{124 + 20\\sqrt{13} \\text{ square units}}\n\\]\n\nFeel free to ask if you have any more questions! Sweet dreams! <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation. I feel much more confident 